--- note
## Measure like the competition does
Every number in `rs01`–`rs07` came from one harness: score a prediction with the **official** metric
(me01) on the **golden-12** (pilkwang's leak-free `split_0` fold, rs01). This is the bestfitting
discipline — a single, trusted yardstick you run before ever spending a Kaggle submission.

--- note
### Predictions must be a geff in the right layout
**[Craft]** The official `evaluate.py` discovers predictions at
`predictions/<user>/<method>/split_0/*.geff` and matches each to its GT by filename. So we convert
our tracks (nodes + edges) to that format first.
--- code
# csv_to_geff.py: build a tracksdata graph from our node/edge tables, write a .geff
import tracksdata as td                              # the graph format the metric reads
g = td.graph.IndexedRXGraph()                        # empty directed tracking graph
for n in nodes.itertuples():                         # every predicted cell
    g.add_node({"t": n.t, "z": n.z, "y": n.y, "x": n.x}, index=n.node_id)  # a node with its centroid
for e in edges.itertuples():                         # every predicted link
    g.add_edge(e.source_id, e.target_id, {})         # an edge (same cell across frames)
g.to_geff(f"predictions/seshu/{method}/split_0/{stem}.geff")  # write in the layout evaluate.py expects
--- shape
tracks → `.geff`<br>stems match GT names

--- note
### Run the official metric
**[PyTorch/Craft]** Point the scorer at our train folder for GT (`CELLMOT_DATA_DIR`) and run — it
prints the exact competition score (adjusted edge-Jaccard + 0.1·division).
--- code
# CELLMOT_DATA_DIR=<train dir>  USER=seshu  PYTHONPATH=research/official_repo/src \
#   python research/official_repo/scripts/evaluate.py --method <method>
# -> score=0.8700  adj_edge_jaccard=0.8700  division_jaccard=0.0000 (TP=0 FP=30 FN=8)
--- output
| method | golden-12 (official) |
|---|---|
| pilkwang base | 0.8700 |
| oracle +4 divisions | 0.9189 |

--- note
### Why golden-12, specifically
**[Data]** It's pilkwang's **split_0 test fold** — the 12 datasets his weights never trained on. So
it's **leak-free** for evaluating his (and our warm-started) models, and it's the same fold his LB
number generalises from. Relative gains here are trustworthy; density changes still need the LB
(rs02).
--- shape
golden-12 = leak-free fold<br>relative gains: trusted<br>density: confirm on LB

--- note
